1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:12,267
On 26 October 2026, OpenAI posted a preprint with a startling title.

2
00:00:12,267 --> 00:00:19,767
A 3-CNF formula is an AND of clauses; each clause is an OR of three literals.

3
00:00:19,767 --> 00:00:28,933
3-SAT asks: can we set the n variables to 0 or 1 so that every clause is true?

4
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:37,367
The obvious method tries all 2^n assignments. Every new variable doubles the work.

5
00:00:37,367 --> 00:00:49,167
Decades of clever algorithms lowered the base, from 2^n to about 1.307^n ... but every one of them is still exponential.

6
00:00:49,167 --> 00:01:00,333
The Exponential Time Hypothesis (Impagliazzo–Paturi, 2001) says this is unavoidable.

7
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:11,400
The preprint claims a deterministic algorithm that counts the solutions of any 3-CNF in subexponential time and polynomial space.

8
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:21,767
That is not P=NP: a time like 2^n/sqrt( log n) still grows faster than every polynomial.

9
00:01:25,167 --> 00:01:34,600
Turn each clause into a polynomial Phi_e: it is 1 when the clause is true and 0 when it is false.

10
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:49,134
The product (1-X_a)X_b(1-X_c) equals 1 only on the single violating assignment (0,1,0).

11
00:01:49,134 --> 00:02:01,500
Multiply over all clauses and add over all 2^n assignments: the result is the number of solutions, #F.

12
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:12,100
The paper first treats formulas where every variable occurs in at most 3 clauses; then prod _e Phi_e has degree at most 3n.

13
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:21,134
(General k-CNF formulas are reduced to this case at the end.) The question is how to evaluate the sum faster.

14
00:02:24,533 --> 00:02:31,600
Suppose the n variables split into r groups, and no clause touches two groups.

15
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:45,200
Then the count factors: count each group on its own and multiply. The cost is about r* 2^2n/r instead of 2^n.

16
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:53,433
Real formulas are tangled: some clauses cross between groups, and the sum no longer factors.

17
00:02:53,433 --> 00:03:04,466
The idea: deform the polynomial so that, at special parameter values, clauses talk across groups only through a few integer summaries.

18
00:03:07,867 --> 00:03:18,700
Introduce a parameter T. In clause e, replace each input X_v by a linear form whose coefficients are polynomials in T.

19
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:33,300
At T=0 every form is X_v again, so H(0)=#F. With coefficient degree at most R, H has degree at most 3nR.

20
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:47,234
The forms are built so that at T=t in 1,...,Q each clause reads its own group of variables, plus a few integer measurements y=Cx of the other groups.

21
00:03:47,234 --> 00:03:57,267
Fix all the measurements vec y, and the groups decouple again: each group is summed on its own, and the results multiply.

22
00:03:57,267 --> 00:04:09,267
Cost: (number of measurement values) x (assignments of one group). Subexponential, if there are few measurements and many groups.

23
00:04:09,267 --> 00:04:15,767
But this only works at T=1,...,Q. We still need H(0).

24
00:04:19,167 --> 00:04:27,867
We can now evaluate H at t=1,...,Q quickly. But the number we want is H(0)=#F.

25
00:04:27,867 --> 00:04:39,167
Q values cannot determine a polynomial of degree up to 3nR: many curves pass through the same points, with different values at 0.

26
00:04:39,167 --> 00:04:48,100
Instead, compute several Taylor coefficients of H at each point: value, slope, curvature, ...

27
00:04:48,100 --> 00:05:03,267
With J coefficients at each of Q points and QJ>deg H, only one polynomial fits: Hermite interpolation recovers H, hence H(0).

28
00:05:03,267 --> 00:05:17,767
Each Taylor coefficient costs extra sums (an inclusion–exclusion over <= J variables). That is cheap only if R<< Q: low degree, many points.

29
00:05:21,167 --> 00:05:37,834
Multiplying a term by (1-T/s) keeps it at T=0 and deletes it at T=s, at the price of one degree. Each term can afford at most R-1 such factors.

30
00:05:37,834 --> 00:05:52,400
Give each occurrence (clause e, variable v) a label: at every point s, the pair (group of v, group assigned to e). A word of Q pairs.

31
00:05:52,400 --> 00:06:04,400
The swap Pi_s exchanges the two entries of pair s. After swapping, the coordinate lives in the clause's own group at point s.

32
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:23,200
Write every coordinate as a measured part plus a local part, using M_0=V_0f(W_0) with W_0=1/2sum _s(I-Pi_s) and deg f<= R-1.

33
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:36,467
The swaps share an eigenbasis. On an eigenvector with j antisymmetric factors, M_0 acts by the number (-1)^jf(j).

34
00:06:36,467 --> 00:06:44,467
Across all basis vectors, j is binomially distributed, with spread about sqrt Q.

35
00:06:44,467 --> 00:06:58,167
Choose f to interpolate (-1)^j on R values of j around the peak. There M_0 acts as the identity; only the thin tails need measuring.

36
00:06:58,167 --> 00:07:12,467
Because deg f<= R-1, each term of M_0 disagrees with the clause's group at no more than R-1 points, so a degree-(R-1) multiplier removes it there.

37
00:07:12,467 --> 00:07:21,400
Taking R>>sqrt Q makes the measured tails tiny, while R<< Q keeps the Taylor step cheap.

38
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:32,300
The total work is a product of three costs. With the chosen parameters each one is 2^o(n).

39
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:48,633
Multiplied together: 2^O(n( log log n)^2/sqrt( log n)) for bounded-occurrence formulas — and only polynomial space, since nothing is tabulated.

40
00:07:48,633 --> 00:08:02,967
General k-CNF formulas: split them into sparse formulas, compress the clauses into O(n) sparse parity tests, and count the common solutions with the same machinery.

41
00:08:02,967 --> 00:08:19,267
A parity test passes when an integer sum is even. The Beigel–Tarui polynomial detects evenness modulo 2^ell, so the count is recovered exactly modulo 2^n+1.

42
00:08:19,267 --> 00:08:31,400
With a sharper recursive analysis, the paper's main theorem reaches exponent O(n log log n/sqrt( log n)) for every fixed k.

43
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:49,367
If the proof holds, it refutes ETH and its relatives SETH, #ETH, Gap-ETH and NSETH, which underpin a large body of conditional lower bounds.

44
00:08:49,367 --> 00:09:01,400
The paper also derives subexponential exact counting for graph colorings and perfect matchings, and a new circuit lower bound for E^NP.

45
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:12,967
It does not settle P vs NP: subexponential is still superpolynomial, and 2^o(n) bounds do not transfer through general reductions.

46
00:09:12,967 --> 00:09:26,533
It is also not a practical solver: with the explicit parameters of the paper's overview, the machinery only switches on when log _2 n is about 10^10.

47
00:09:26,533 --> 00:09:36,667
Status: a preprint with a reported Lean formalization, now being examined by experts, including the hypothesis's originators.
